  "live_records": {
    "q-2436": {
      "dom_id": "step-q2436",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The volume $V$, in gallons, of water in a pool $t$ minutes after the pool starts filling can be modeled by the function $V(t)=5\\sqrt{t}+2.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change of the water volume when $t=4$ minutes?",
      "worked_solution": "The instantaneous rate of change of the water volume when $t=4$ minutes is equal to ${V}^{′}(4).$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\sqrt{t}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{t}^{\\frac{1}{2}}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{-\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=4,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the water volume when $t=4$ minutes is $\\frac{5}{4}$ gallons per minute.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
